uld bring the NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the ~$7.8 million full-sourcing estimate, as procurement structure materially moves the final price.\",\n   \"The MarketScale report cites constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, noting that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers about $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation; the bank estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU in volume, and notes a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content in NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million per rack, a 435% increase over the prior generation, driven by a threefold rise in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage; memory's share of the bill of materials rose to 25–30% on VR200 from 5–10% on GB200, while the GPU share fell from about 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\",\n     \"new-memory-price\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 racks are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\