per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND; memory's share of the bill of materials rises to 25-30% on the VR200 from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share falls from roughly 63% to 51%, and a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\",\n     \"memory_prices_2027\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that contract DDR5 memory pricing has climbed sharply, that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that the AI buildout and consumer-device memory costs are now tied to the same supply squeeze.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"memory_prices_2027\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 70,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10%, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\",\n   \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\",\n   \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand has pushed contract DDR5 pricing sharply higher, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move rack pricing by $1+ million.\"\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. Memory is the driver of the increase: about $2 million of memory content per rack, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND. Memory rises from 5-10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25-30% on VR200, while the GPU's share falls from roughly 63% to 51%. Reported by MarketScale from Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, with the article stating the GPUs themselves are not the driver of the rack price increase.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"The item reports that first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter; no direct attribution for the schedule is given in the article.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, and the article states procurement structure can move final rack pricing by more than $1 million per rack.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 71,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell to about 51% from roughly 63%.\"\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates, in a bill-of-materials analysis reported by MarketScale, that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\",\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to the Morgan Stanley Research analysis reported by MarketScale, first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin (VR200) racks are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter; the item gives no Nvidia confirmation of the schedule.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a roughly threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems; memory's share of the bill of materials rises to 25–30% on VR200 from 5–10% on GB200, while the GPU's share falls from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\",\n     \"new-mem-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin VR200 rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus its roughly $7.8 million estimate, showing that procurement structure materially moves the per-rack figure.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports, citing constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand as the force behind rising AI rack prices, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-mem-1\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 72,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   \"In Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis, memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack is about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose from 5–10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n   \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the ~$7.8 million full-sourcing estimate, as procurement structure materially moves the final price.\",\n   \"The MarketScale report cites constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, noting that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, with NVIDIA charging about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume; a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems; memory rose from 5 to 10 percent of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on VR200 while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\",\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley's analysis states NVIDIA's Vera Rubin first shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev4\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports that supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory is constrained against AI-driven demand, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, tying the AI buildout and laptop memory prices to the same supply squeeze.\",\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n }\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
