2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose from 5–10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n   \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the ~$7.8 million full-sourcing estimate, as procurement structure materially moves the final price.\",\n   \"The MarketScale report cites constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, noting that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume. In the bank's bill-of-materials analysis, memory content is about $2 million per rack (a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a roughly threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage), memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5–10% on the GB200 to 25–30% on the VR200, and the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to the Morgan Stanley Research analysis reported by MarketScale, first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"The item reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is the force behind the higher Vera Rubin rack price and is raising component costs across the market; contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules for NVIDIA's Vera Rubin rack could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the roughly $7.8 million estimated purchase price, indicating procurement structure materially moves the final number.\",\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 67,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell to about 51% from roughly 63%.\"\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research's bill-of-materials analysis estimates Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. Memory content per rack is put at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, on a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage; as a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5–10% on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200 while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-mem-price\",\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research's breakdown has Nvidia's first Vera Rubin shipments scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
